{{> head}}
{{> nav}}
<main id="main">

  <section class="section hero scheme-dark case-hero" aria-labelledby="ps-title">
    <div class="container hero__inner">
      <div class="hero__copy">
        <div class="stack">
          <p class="eyebrow" data-reveal>01 Strategy</p>
          <h1 class="h1" id="ps-title" data-split>Product strategy for startups, from idea to a clear MVP <span class="mark">scope<svg class="mark__svg" viewBox="0 0 240 24" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" data-draw><path d="M4 15 C 46 8, 92 19, 136 12 S 210 7, 236 13"/></svg></span></h1>
          <p class="text-lg" data-reveal>You have an early-stage idea and too many possible features. We turn it into a buildable MVP: what to build first, what to leave out, and why. AI agents do the research legwork; a senior designer makes every call.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="actions actions--center" data-reveal>
          <a class="btn btn--lime" href="{{calendar}}" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span class="btn__label"><span data-text="Book a Call">Book a Call</span></span></a>
          <a class="btn btn--ghost-dark" href="/work/lula/"><span class="btn__label"><span data-text="See an example">See an example</span></span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section scheme-light" aria-labelledby="ps-what-title">
    <div class="container split-rows">
      <div class="ai-def">
        <h2 class="h2" id="ps-what-title" data-split>What is product strategy for an early-stage startup?</h2>
        <div class="text-lg prose" data-reveal>
          <p>Product strategy for an early-stage startup is the work of deciding what the first version of a product must do, for whom, and what it should leave out. It turns an idea into an MVP scope: a short, ranked list of features, the core user flows, and the criteria that say when each part is done. Good MVP scoping makes the first build smaller, clearer and easier to test.</p>
          <p>It is the first step of our process, 01 Strategy, before 02 Design, 03 Development and 04 Measure &amp; Optimise. The whole path is on <a class="text-link" href="/idea-to-build-ready/">from idea to build-ready</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="ai-def">
        <h2 class="h2" id="ps-do-title" data-split>What do we do?</h2>
        <div class="text-lg prose" data-reveal>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Research synthesis:</strong> we pull together what you already know, plus the market, into one picture.</li>
            <li><strong>Competitive scan:</strong> what similar products offer, and where yours can stand apart.</li>
            <li><strong>Jobs and personas:</strong> who the product is for and what they are trying to get done.</li>
            <li><strong>MVP scope:</strong> what goes into the first version and, just as important, what stays out.</li>
            <li><strong>Success criteria:</strong> how you will know the MVP is doing its job.</li>
            <li><strong>The first flows:</strong> the core user journeys, written out step by step, ready for design.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section scheme-coral" aria-labelledby="ps-ai-title">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-title center narrow">
        <h2 class="h2" id="ps-ai-title" data-split>How does AI-native product strategy work?</h2>
        <p class="text-lg" data-reveal>AI explores wide and fast. A designer decides. It is the same human-in-the-loop way we work <a class="text-link" href="/ai-design/">across all our design work</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="cycle" data-reveal-group>
        <li class="cycle__step" data-reveal>
          <span class="cycle__num">01</span>
          <h3 class="cycle__title">Map the field</h3>
          <p><strong>AI</strong> agents synthesise research and scan competitors in days, not weeks.</p>
          <p><strong>Designer</strong> checks what is evidence and what is still an assumption.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="cycle__step" data-reveal>
          <span class="cycle__num">02</span>
          <h3 class="cycle__title">Set the scope</h3>
          <p><strong>AI</strong> drafts the long list of possible features.</p>
          <p><strong>Designer</strong> ranks every feature and sets the MVP scope.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="cycle__step" data-reveal>
          <span class="cycle__num">03</span>
          <h3 class="cycle__title">Write it down</h3>
          <p><strong>AI</strong> helps shape the brief and the flow specs.</p>
          <p><strong>Designer</strong> records each decision with its reason, so nobody has to guess later.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="cycle__step" data-reveal>
          <span class="cycle__num">04</span>
          <h3 class="cycle__title">Hand over</h3>
          <p><strong>AI</strong> turns each milestone into acceptance criteria.</p>
          <p><strong>Designer</strong> walks your team through the scope and the "not now" list.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section scheme-light" aria-labelledby="ps-get-title">
    <div class="container ai-def">
      <h2 class="h2" id="ps-get-title" data-split>What do you get?</h2>
      <div class="text-lg prose" data-reveal>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>An MVP brief</strong> your team and your investors can read in one sitting.</li>
          <li><strong>A ranked feature list</strong>, with a "not now" list of what was left out and why.</li>
          <li><strong>A flow map</strong> of the core user journeys.</li>
          <li><strong>Acceptance criteria per milestone</strong>, so design and development know when a part is done.</li>
          <li><strong>A decision log:</strong> every call, with its reason.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>From there we can test the riskiest flow as a <a class="text-link" href="/prototyping/">clickable prototype</a> or go straight into <a class="text-link" href="/ui-ux-design/">UI/UX design</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section scheme-dark" aria-labelledby="ps-example-title">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-title narrow">
        <p class="tagline" data-reveal>Example</p>
        <h2 class="h2" id="ps-example-title" data-split>From research to MVP brief for Lula</h2>
        <p class="text-lg" data-reveal>For <a class="text-link" href="/work/lula/">Lula</a>, an AI bedtime-story app, research synthesis to MVP brief took about a week. Our designer ranked 28 features and set the MVP scope. One decision shaped the product: the first personalised story comes before the paywall, so parents hear what they are paying for.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stats stats--3" data-reveal-group>
        <div class="stat" data-reveal><span class="stat__num">~1 wk</span><p>from research to the MVP brief</p></div>
        <div class="stat" data-reveal><span class="stat__num">28</span><p>features ranked by our designer</p></div>
        <div class="stat" data-reveal><span class="stat__num">6</span><p>user flows specced, from onboarding to profile</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section scheme-royal" aria-labelledby="ps-faq-title">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-title narrow">
        <h2 class="h2" id="ps-faq-title" data-split>FAQs</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="faq__list" data-reveal>
        <details class="faq__item" data-accordion open>
          <summary class="faq__q"><span>How long does product strategy take?</span><img class="faq__icon" src="/assets/img/figma/chevron-up.svg" width="32" height="32" alt=""></summary>
          <div class="faq__a" data-accordion-body><div><p>It depends on scope. For Lula, research synthesis to MVP brief took about a week.</p></div></div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq__item" data-accordion>
          <summary class="faq__q"><span>Do we need research first?</span><img class="faq__icon" src="/assets/img/figma/chevron-up.svg" width="32" height="32" alt=""></summary>
          <div class="faq__a" data-accordion-body><div><p>No. We start from what you have: notes, interviews, a pitch deck or just the idea. We say clearly which parts of the scope rest on evidence and which are assumptions to test.</p></div></div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq__item" data-accordion>
          <summary class="faq__q"><span>What if we already have designs?</span><img class="faq__icon" src="/assets/img/figma/chevron-up.svg" width="32" height="32" alt=""></summary>
          <div class="faq__a" data-accordion-body><div><p>Good. We use them as input, check them against the MVP scope, and tell you what to keep, change or set aside for later.</p></div></div>
        </details>
        <details class="faq__item" data-accordion>
          <summary class="faq__q"><span>Is this only for AI products?</span><img class="faq__icon" src="/assets/img/figma/chevron-up.svg" width="32" height="32" alt=""></summary>
          <div class="faq__a" data-accordion-body><div><p>No. AI-native describes how we work, not what you build. Any startup with an early-stage idea can use it.</p></div></div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

